REGIONAL REGULATION OF MAGELANG REGENCY

NUMBER 2 YEAR 2016

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN AGAINST ACTS OF VIOLENCE AND DISCRIMINATION

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

THE REGENT OF MAGELANG,

Considering:

a. that protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination aims to realize constitutional rights and human rights in accordance with the values of Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution;
b. that based on Law Number 23 Year 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, the Regional Government together with the community is obliged to undertake prevention, protection, and recovery efforts for women and children victims of violence and discrimination;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Protection of Women and Children Against Acts of Violence and Discrimination;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 7 Year 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 4 Year 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
4. Law Number 39 Year 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 Year 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 Year 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 Year 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
6. Law Number 13 Year 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);
7. Law Number 23 Year 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
8. Law Number 21 Year 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
9. Law Number 25 Year 2009 concerning Public Services (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2009 Number 112, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5038);
10. Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 Year 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
11. Government Regulation Number 4 Year 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
12. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 3 Year 2009 concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children (Regional Gazette of Central Java Province Year 2009 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Central Java Province Number 20);
13. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 7 Year 2013 concerning the Implementation of Child Protection (Regional Gazette of Central Java Province Year 2013 Number 7, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Central Java Province Number 53);

With Mutual Approval

THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF MAGELANG REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF MAGELANG

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN AGAINST ACTS OF VIOLENCE AND DISCRIMINATION

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. victim protection;
e. empowerment; and
f. integration.

Article 3

The objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence and discrimination are to:
a. prevent acts of violence and discrimination against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of violence, discrimination and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect women and children from acts of violence and discrimination in obtaining their legitimate rights constitutionally;
d. protect, provide a sense of security for women and children;
e. provide services and protection to women and child victims of acts of violence and discrimination, reporters, and witnesses; and
f. facilitate and mediate domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.

CHAPTER III

PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE AND DISCRIMINATION

Article 4

1. To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence and discrimination against women and children, the Regional Government carries out empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government carries out the following efforts:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. facilitating the fulfillment of employment opportunities for women;
d. encouraging community participation and concern for the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, educational institutions and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening an integrated service system for the protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination in every village.

Article 5

1. Prevention of acts of violence and discrimination against women and children as referred to in Article 4, shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. people's welfare; and
g. peace and order.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE AND DISCRIMINATION

Article 6

Women and children victims of acts of violence and discrimination shall receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance;
j. the right to respect for survival, growth and development;
k. the right to basic services;
l. the right to equal protection;
m. the right to be free from various stigma; and
n. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 8

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence and discrimination as referred to in Article 7 include:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid services;
d. repatriation services;
e. social rehabilitation and reintegration services; and
f. education services for children who are victims of acts of violence and discrimination.
2. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. women's empowerment and child protection; and
e. mental and spiritual.
3. Further provisions regarding procedures for handling services for women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 10

1. Obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 10 letter a, include:
a. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination;
b. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
g. providing recovery and social reintegration services;
h. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
i. providing Human Resources in the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence and discrimination.
2. In order to carry out obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent shall establish programs and action activities for the protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 11

Obligations and responsibilities of the Village Government as referred to in Article 10 letter b, include:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination established by the government;
b. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination;
c. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination;
d. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination in accordance with village financial capacity;
e. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination; and
f. encouraging and increasing community participation.

Article 12

1. The community shall participate in the protection of women and children victims of violence and discrimination, both individually and in groups.
2. The role of the community as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, women and child protection institutions, social welfare institutions, community organizations, educational institutions, mass media, and the business sector.
3. The role of the community in the implementation of protection of women and children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:
a. providing information through socialization and education regarding the protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination;
b. providing input in the formulation of policies related to the protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination;
c. reporting to the competent authorities if violations of acts of violence and discrimination against women and children occur;
d. actively participating in the process of rehabilitation and social reintegration for women and children victims of acts of violence and discrimination;
e. conducting monitoring and supervision of the protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination;
f. providing facilities and infrastructure in order to support the protection of women and children from acts of violence and discrimination;
g. actively participating in eliminating negative labeling of women and children victims of acts of violence and discrimination; and
h. providing space for women and children to participate and express their opinions.

Article 18

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from violence as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government shall cooperate with:
a. the Government;
b. the Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Governments;
d. the Village Government;
e. the Community; and
f. the Mass Media.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be set forth in the form of a Joint Agreement in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 19

1. The Regional Government shall establish partnerships with the business sector in the protection of women and children from violence and discrimination.
2. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted through:
a. notification of employment opportunity information for women victims of violence;
b. education and training for women victims of violence;
c. educational assistance for women and children victims of violence who have been withdrawn from their education;
d. fostering and enhancing economic independence of women victims of violence; and
e. elimination of the worst forms of child labor.
4. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), shall be set forth in the form of an agreement in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
